  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Task 4 result — the family Omega_c[w] (x) Omega_c[w'] over (w1, w2, w1', w2') (the two cells above)\n\n**Set-up.** With a = 1/w1, s = 1/w2 (a1, s1 for w; a2, s2 for w') the covector is Omega_c = gaussCov[{a, -a s, a + s}], polynomial in (a, s); the blocks A^(c,c')(a1,s1,a2,s2) are therefore matrices of POLYNOMIALS (no denominators to clear): [2,1,0] entries of degree <= 3 in each variable (<= 12 terms), [3,1,0] degree <= 5 (<= 22 terms). Checked: the symbolic phi at (1/w1, 1/w2, 1/w1', 1/w2') equals the numeric phi of Omega_c[w] (x) Omega_c[w'] exactly. The w2 -> infinity (s -> 0) limit of Omega_c is computed, not guessed: gaussCov[{a, 0, a}] = e1 exp(a E12) exp(a E23) (the guess gaussCov[{1/w1, 0, 1/w1}] was right).\n\n**(i) Generic ranks** (rank at random rational points, max of 3; all 40 random generic points give the same ΣP): [2,1,0] r = 5,3,3,2 (sum 13), **sum P = 22** (40 of 40 samples); [3,1,0] r = 9,6,6,6 (sum 27), **sum P = 54** (40 of 40).\n\n**(iii) Sub-family tables** (rational members; 4 members each, all members agree unless stated; r per block (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1); the printed cell has P per block):\n\n| sub-family | [2,1,0]: r | sum r | sum P | [3,1,0]: r | sum r | sum P |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| generic (4 parameters) | 5,3,3,2 | 13 | **22** | 9,6,6,6 | 27 | **54** |\n| w' = w (the vacuum) | 5,3,3,2 | 13 | 18 | 8,6,6,6 | 26 | 40 |\n| **w1' = w1 (common w1; 3 parameters)** | 5,3,3,2 | 13 | **18** | 8,6,6,6 | 26 | **40** |\n| w2' = w2; w1' = w2; w2' = w1; swap (w1' = w2 & w2' = w1); w1 = w2; w1' = w2' | 5,3,3,2 | 13 | 22 (generic) | 9,6,6,6 | 27 | 54 (generic) |\n| w1' = w1 = w2' | 5,3,3,2 | 13 | 18 | 8,6,6,6 | 26 | 40 |\n| w2 -> infinity (s1 = 0) | 5,3,3,2 | 13 | 20 | 9,6,6,6 | 27 | 54 |\n| w2' -> infinity (s2 = 0) | 3,3,2,2 | 10 | 14 | 6,6,6,6 | 24 | 45 |\n| w2, w2' -> infinity | 3,3,2,2 | 10 | 12 | 6,6,6,6 | 24 | 45 |\n| w1' = w1, w2 -> infinity | 4,3,3,2 | 12 | 14 | 6,6,6,6 | 24 | 27 |\n| w1' = w1, w2' -> infinity | 2,3,2,2 | 9 | **10** | 3,6,3,6 | 18 | **21** |\n| w1' = w1, w2, w2' -> infinity (1 parameter) | 2,2,2,2 | 8 | **8 (exact: P = r)** | 2,3,3,6 | 14 | **14 (exact: P = r)** |\n| w2' -> infinity with w1' = w2 or w1 = w2 | 3,3,2,2 | 10 | 14 | 6,6,6,6 | 24 | 45 |\n| w1, w1' -> infinity (both covectors = e1) | 1,1,1,1 | 4 | 4 | same | 4 | 4 |\n\n* **The count depends on the coincidence w1' = w1 and on the w2 -> infinity limits, not on the other coincidences**: w1' = w1 alone lowers P from 22 to 18 / 54 to 40 (the value of the vacuum w' = w itself); the coincidences w2' = w2, w1' = w2, w2' = w1, w1 = w2, w1' = w2' do nothing. w2' -> infinity lowers P to 14 / 45 and the rank sum to 10 / 24 (a matrix-rank drop), the combination w1' = w1 and w2' -> infinity gives 10 / 21, and w1' = w1 with both w2, w2' -> infinity gives the exact counts **8 ([2,1,0]) and 14 ([3,1,0])** (lower bound = upper bound).\n* **Minimal count over the family**: attained at the sub-families w1' = w1 with w2' (and w2) -> infinity: 10 / 21 (2 parameters w1, w2) and 8 / 14 (1 parameter). Over the open 4-parameter family the count is the generic 22 / 54. These are upper bounds; the exact minimal count is a tensor-rank problem that was not attempted.\n* **Product test on each sub-family**: no sub-family count is the product (site-1 count) x (site-2 count) of the one-site counts: e.g. w1' = w1 gives 18 / 40, the one-site counts are 5 x 5 or 4 x 4 (adjoint) and 7 x 7 or 5 x 5 ([3,1,0]); only the very degenerate 8 / 14 are close to the best-x-best products 16 / 25, and these live on a 1-parameter family.\n\n**(ii) Rank-drop loci** (common irreducible factors of det(P A Q), P, Q random small integer r x nMin / nMin x r matrices, two draws; Groebner bases were NOT used — the block minors are too large; hypersurface components only, codimension >= 2 pieces are probed only by the sub-family tables):\n\n| block | [2,1,0] | [3,1,0] |\n|---|---|---|\n| (0,0) | a1, a2, s2 and an irreducible hypersurface of degree 14 (163 terms) in (a1,s1,a2,s2); numerically verified: rank drops 5 -> 4 on it | rank 9; determinant too large: restriction to a random line gives linear factors and one high-degree factor (degree ~30: not stable between lines, not reliable) — coordinate hyperplanes among them |\n| (0,1) | a2 | a2 |\n| (1,0) | a2, s2 | a2 |\n| (1,1) | a2 | a1, a2, **9 a1 - 44 a2** (a t-dependent ratio w1'/w1 = 44/9), 2134 a1^2 - 1188 a1 a2 + 279 a2^2, 210 a1^2 - 665 a1 a2 - 93 s2 (a1 - a2), and a degree-4 hypersurface in (a1,a2,s2) |\n\nThe 'obvious' coincidences w1 = w1', w2 = w2', ... are NOT rank-drop loci for [2,1,0] (the matrix ranks on them are generic although P drops on w1' = w1: the count drop there is a drop of the 2-form ranks of the factors, i.e. a decomposability locus, not a matrix-rank drop; for [3,1,0] the (0,0) rank does drop 9 -> 8 on w1' = w1). The rank drops sit on the coordinate hyperplanes (w -> infinity limits: a = 0, s2 = 0) and on t-dependent special ratios and surfaces; the genuine content is mostly the sub-family structure listed in the table.",
